(N'东北民谣', N'15', N'bilibili', N'“Ode to My Northern Home”  Beneath the wind that stirs the golden grain, Sweet blossoms rise o’er fields of gentle rain. This dark, rich earth hath reared both sire and dame, Through winter’s snows, we warm by hearth’s bright flame.  The heavens wide, where clouds like spirits roam, And rivers sing beside the woodland’s home. We feast on fare of sauerkraut and blood, And dance the reels till joy becomes our flood.  O Northland fair, thou art my cradle’s ground, Thy pipes and horns with merry song resound. Where comrades meet, we share the brimming cup, ’Neath silver moon, our hearts are lifted up.  O Northland fair, my birth and spirit’s claim, In thee I stand, in thee I bear my name. Thy people’s love runs fierce and strong and wide, By hearth and snow, my soul shall e’er abide. [doge][doge][doge]', N'170', N'2025-11-16'),
